f 17592186045417] [17592186362073 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6a1f27a1-0eda-517d-a4eb-016e3810f8d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361305 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362085 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7ce9a233-37ed-5628-bbcf-882c2c09d5d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361456 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362188 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e828baa6-f3ec-5068-b0c9-1c1c3f9d8a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362188 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular parallelepiped has a volume of $125\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}.$ Using the method of Lagrange multipliers, determine the minimum sum of the lengths of its edges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1ec51af1-dbfa-5fa9-8f74-3c330eb163be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362342 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4 &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186364649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1071 "A rectangular metal tank without a lid should hold $256\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}$ of liquid. Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the tank's minimum possible surface area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9021d181-6a6e-589c-8096-c7ad92921266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364648 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z &=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z &=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y &=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z) &=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y) &=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364650 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186364654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364649 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6ae58a56-6faf-52c9-9561-c87d35603ea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1080 "243\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364650 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f027768c-f196-5e3f-adf4-7554b2177b3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364651 #edb/ref 1080 "162\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364651 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364651 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f96459e6-4979-5896-b4e6-40cb50e3fdd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186364652 #edb/ref 1080 "128\\,